          id: '103_3_15_1',
          question: 'Examine the ancient Greek civilization: city-states and legacy in science and philosophy.',
          answer:
            'INTRODUCTION\nAncient Greek civilization (c. 800–31 BCE) is arguably the single most influential civilization in world history. In the span of a few centuries, the Greeks developed democracy, rational philosophy, systematic science, history as a discipline, realistic art, and the concept of the individual as a political and rational being. These Greek achievements, transmitted to Rome, preserved through the Islamic world during Europe\'s Middle Ages, and rediscovered in the Renaissance, became the foundations of Western civilization.\n\nTHE CITY-STATE (POLIS)\nThe fundamental political unit of Greek civilization was the polis—the city-state. Unlike the great river-valley empires of Mesopotamia and Egypt, Greece developed as a fragmented system of small, independent city-states (Athens, Sparta, Corinth, Thebes, etc.), each with its own government, laws, and culture. The mountainous Greek geography, which isolated communities from each other, encouraged this political fragmentation. Each polis had an acropolis (fortified hilltop), an agora (public marketplace and meeting place), temples, and public buildings. The polis became the context for Greek political experimentation.\n\nDEMOCRACY AT ATHENS\nAthens developed the world\'s first democracy. Cleisthenes\' reforms (508 BCE) created a system in which all adult male Athenian citizens could participate directly in government—voting in the Assembly, serving as jurors, and holding public offices. Pericles (461–429 BCE) further democratized the system, paying citizens for jury service to enable the poor to participate. Though limited by modern standards (excluding women, slaves, and foreigners), Athenian democracy was a revolutionary political innovation that established the principle of popular self-government.\n\nSPARTA\nSparta offered a contrasting model: a military oligarchy in which all male citizens were full-time warriors, children were trained from age 7 for war, and material culture was suppressed in favor of military virtue. Spartan women had unusual freedom compared to Athenian women. The Sparta-Athens rivalry dominated classical Greek politics, culminating in the catastrophic Peloponnesian War (431–404 BCE).\n\nPHILOSOPHY\nGreek philosophy began with the pre-Socratics (6th–5th centuries BCE), who sought rational (non-mythological) explanations for natural phenomena. Thales of Miletus proposed water as the fundamental substance; Heraclitus emphasized constant change ("you cannot step into the same river twice"); Democritus proposed atoms as the fundamental units of matter—remarkably anticipating modern atomic theory. Socrates (470–399 BCE) redirected philosophy from cosmology to ethics and the examined life ("The unexamined life is not worth living"). Plato (428–348 BCE) developed a comprehensive philosophy encompassing metaphysics (Theory of Forms), epistemology, politics (the Republic), and education. Aristotle (384–322 BCE) systematized virtually all human knowledge—biology, physics, logic, ethics, politics, aesthetics—establishing frameworks that dominated Western thought for nearly 2,000 years.\n\nSCIENCE AND MATHEMATICS\nThe Greeks pioneered systematic rational inquiry into nature. Euclid\'s Elements (c. 300 BCE) established geometry as a deductive system that remained the standard for 2,000 years. Archimedes (c. 287–212 BCE) made fundamental contributions to mathematics (calculation of pi), physics (principles of the lever and buoyancy), and engineering. Hippocrates (c. 460–370 BCE) established medicine as a rational practice separate from religion—the Hippocratic Oath is still used today. Aristarchus proposed a heliocentric model of the solar system, 1,800 years before Copernicus.\n\nLITERATURE AND DRAMA\nGreek literature began with Homer\'s epics—the Iliad and Odyssey (c. 8th century BCE)—which established the heroes of Greek mythology. Drama—both tragedy (Sophocles\' Oedipus Rex, Euripides\' Medea, Aeschylus\' Oresteia) and comedy (Aristophanes\' The Clouds, The Birds)—was invented in Athens as a religious and civic institution. History as a discipline was created by Herodotus (the "Father of History") and Thucydides, who applied critical analysis to the past.\n\nART AND ARCHITECTURE\nGreek art pursued the ideal beauty of the human form—contrasting with Egyptian conventionalism. The Parthenon, the Nike of Samothrace, the Venus de Milo, and the Discobolus represent the classical ideal of perfect human proportion. Greek three orders of architecture—Doric, Ionic, and Corinthian—have been the basis of Western architectural vocabulary from Rome to Washington D.C.\n\nCONCLUSION\nThe legacy of ancient Greek civilization in democracy, philosophy, science, literature, drama, and art cannot be overstated. Through Alexander the Great\'s conquests (which spread Greek culture from Egypt to India in the Hellenistic Age), through Rome (which adopted and transmitted Greek culture), and through the Renaissance revival, Greek thought has shaped the modern world more profoundly than any other ancient civilization. The fundamental Western conviction that the universe is rationally ordered and can be understood through human reason is, at its core, a Greek inheritance.',
