          id: '103_4_15_3',
          question: 'Examine the Crusades: causes, course, and consequences.',
          answer:
            'INTRODUCTION\nThe Crusades were a series of religious military expeditions (1096–1291 CE) launched primarily by Western European Christians to recover the Holy Land from Muslim control. They were one of the most significant events of the medieval period—simultaneously expressing the religious zeal of medieval Christendom and having profound long-term consequences for European society, commerce, culture, and the relationship between the Christian and Islamic worlds. Historians have identified up to nine major Crusades, though the movement extended beyond these official expeditions.\n\nCAUSES\nThe immediate cause was Pope Urban II\'s speech at the Council of Clermont (1095 CE), which called for a holy war to liberate Jerusalem from the Seljuk Turks, who had recently defeated the Byzantine Emperor at Manzikert (1071) and cut off access to Christian holy places. The underlying causes were: (1) Religious: Jerusalem and Palestine were the holiest sites of Christendom; the Pope promised indulgences (remission of sin) for those who participated; the religious fervor of 11th-century Europe was intense; (2) Political: Pope Urban II sought to reunite Eastern and Western Christianity and strengthen papal authority; (3) Social: younger sons of nobles (who could not inherit under primogeniture) needed outlets; population growth created pressure; (4) Economic: Italian merchant cities (Venice, Genoa, Pisa) saw opportunities for profitable trade with the East.',
